Free the compiled pattern and the translation buffer when grep() unwinds.

do_grep() and do_regexpr() (src/main/grep.c) compile the pattern with
malloc() and free it after the loop over the text vector.  An input
string that cannot be translated to the wide-character or UTF-8 form
draws a warning inside that loop, which options(warn = 2) turns into an
error, and a failed R allocation inside the loop errors as well; either
unwind skips the free.  The loop now runs under a CTXT_CCODE context
whose cleanup releases the pattern, as read.dcf() does for its regexps.

The translation helpers in src/main/sysutils.c (translateToNative(),
translateToUTF8(), translateToWchar()) signalled the "unable to
translate" warning while the caller's malloc()ed string buffer was still
live, so the same unwind leaked the buffer too.  They now build the
message, release the buffer and then signal; installTrChar() asks for
the plain translation and warns itself after install().

Found by OSS-Fuzz: testcase 6188059406958592 (libFuzzer_r_load,
Direct-leak, R_AllocStringBuffer <- translateToWchar <- Rf_wtransChar2
<- do_grep).  The harness caps the vector heap at 1 GB; a long run of
the load target drifted towards it, and the allocation errors inside
the grepl() that load() runs on the file's magic number leaked 103
compiled patterns and 102 translation buffers over the run.

Generated against a tree with the earlier patches applied (0032 touches
installTrChar() as well), so it has to stay after them in the sequence.

Upstream status: r-devel/r-svn PR #400, filed 2026-10-23 (no Bugzilla
report yet).
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk.

